Description: notify systemd once the daemons are ready
 seaf-server, seaf-fuse, the go fileserver, the notification server and
 seafevents send READY=1 to $NOTIFY_SOCKET (the sd_notify protocol, without
 libsystemd) once their sockets are bound or the mount is set up, so Type=notify
 units become active when they can serve and units ordered after them start
 then. Without $NOTIFY_SOCKET nothing else changes, except that two listeners
 bind before their thread starts: seaf-server's built-in http server, and
 seafevents' http server, whose bind failure now exits seafevents instead of
 leaving it running without its API.
Author: dionysius <dragon.dionysius@gmail.com>
Forwarded: not-yet
